root = TaxonomyBrowser(
  "Explaining Techniques",
  [
    analogyWithItsLimits,
    concreteBeforeAbstract,
    refutationFixTheMisconception,
    nonExamples,
    dualCodingWordsDiagram,
  ],
)

analogyWithItsLimits = TaxonomyCategory(
  "Analogy (with Its Limits)",
  "New idea ← familiar idea",
  "Link",
  "mauve",
  "An analogy links a new idea to one the user already knows (a workspace is like a shared drive folder), and a good one says where it breaks down.",
  "'A workspace is like a shared drive folder: everyone in it sees what's inside.' The user instantly borrows a whole structure they already understand. But every analogy breaks somewhere, and users will carry the broken part over too. So add the limit: 'Unlike a folder, a workspace also has its own billing and settings.'",
  "A borrowed map of a similar city: very useful, as long as you mark the streets that are different.",
  "Fast understanding by borrowing a known structure",
  ["One familiar comparison per idea", "Stating where the analogy breaks"],
  ["Analogies from a world users don't know"],
)

concreteBeforeAbstract = TaxonomyCategory(
  "Concrete Before Abstract",
  "Worked example, then the rule",
  "FileText",
  "sky",
  "Concrete before abstract: start with a worked example (Anna submits a 7,000-baht claim) and state the general rule after it.",
  "'Claims over 5,000 baht go through two approval levels' is abstract. 'Anna submits a 7,000-baht taxi claim. It goes to her manager, Ben, then to Finance, because it's over 5,000 baht' is concrete. Start with the concrete story, then state the rule; the reader now has a hook to hang it on. Worked examples like this are one of the best-researched ways to teach new ideas.",
  "Seeing one goal replayed before hearing the offside rule.",
  "Giving abstract rules a concrete hook",
  ["Named people, real amounts, realistic cases", "The rule stated after the example"],
  ["Rules with no example at all"],
)

refutationFixTheMisconception = TaxonomyCategory(
  "Refutation (Fix the Misconception)",
  "State it · reject it · explain the truth",
  "CircleX",
  "red",
  "Refutation text corrects a misconception by stating the wrong belief, saying clearly that it is wrong, then explaining what is true (archiving is not deleting).",
  "Users arrive with wrong beliefs, and simply stating the truth often doesn't replace them. Refutation text names the belief directly: 'You might think archiving deletes a project. It doesn't. Archiving hides the project from lists; you can restore it at any time, and nothing is lost.' Naming the misconception makes the reader notice the conflict and update their model.",
  "A sign saying 'This is not the exit', right where everyone thinks it is.",
  "Replacing wrong beliefs, not just adding facts",
  ["Common misconceptions from support tickets", "Explicit 'this is not true' statements"],
  ["Listing every possible wrong idea"],
)

nonExamples = TaxonomyCategory(
  "Non-Examples",
  "What it is not",
  "Ban",
  "yellow",
  "Non-examples show what the concept is not, which sharpens its boundaries (a draft is not a submitted claim).",
  "To learn what a 'submitted claim' is, it helps to see things that look similar but aren't: a saved draft (not sent to anyone), a claim returned for changes (sent back, no longer waiting for approval). Showing the near-misses draws the edge of the concept, which is exactly where users make mistakes.",
  "Learning what a dog is gets easier once someone shows you a wolf and a fox.",
  "Sharp boundaries between similar ideas",
  ["Look-alike states and objects", "Near-misses users confuse"],
  ["Unrelated things nobody would confuse"],
)

dualCodingWordsDiagram = TaxonomyCategory(
  "Dual Coding (Words + Diagram)",
  "Say it and show it",
  "Shapes",
  "green",
  "Dual coding: pairing words with a diagram helps people understand and remember better than words alone.",
  "'An organisation has workspaces; a workspace has projects; permissions set on a workspace flow down to its projects' is hard to hold in your head as a sentence. The same idea as a simple box diagram, with an arrow showing permissions flowing down, plus the sentence, gives the reader two routes into memory. Keep diagrams simple: boxes, arrows, a few labels.",
  "Directions plus a sketch map, instead of directions alone.",
  "Structure you can see",
  ["Simple box-and-arrow diagrams next to the text"],
  ["Decorative images that don't show the structure"],
)
